      "final_message": "I rewrote `solution.py` so `semver_compare(a, b)` follows the README, and both public tests pass.\n\n- **Parsing:** `fullmatch` against `(0|[1-9][0-9]*)` for each of the three parts. This allows only ASCII digits and rejects leading zeros like `\"01\"`. It also rejects strings with a trailing newline, a pre-release or build suffix, or the wrong number of parts; any of these raises `ValueError`.\n- **Type check:** any argument that isn't a string, including `bytes`, raises `TypeError`.\n- **Comparison:** the versions are compared as integer tuples `(major, minor, patch)`, returning -1, 0 or 1. Because the parts are numbers rather than text, `\"1.10.0\"` counts as greater than `\"1.9.0\"`.\n\nI also ran some extra checks by hand beyond the two public tests, and they all behaved as expected. These included invalid inputs like `\"1.2.3\\n\"`, non-ASCII digits, a leading space and `\"1.2.3-rc\"`, plus a few multi-digit comparisons.\n\nDONE: Rewrote semver_compare to validate strict MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH input (ValueError for bad strings, TypeError for non-strings) and compare as integer tuples; both public tests pass.",
